<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Peru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Charcas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was the name of the highland territory of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Charcas in the Spanish empire, corresponding roughly to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> until 1776 and then to the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. It declared independence in 1825 as the Republic of Bolivia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-peru.jpg" alt="Upper Peru" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: previous version Ignaciogavira; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its armies and generals freed not only Venezuela, New Granada and Quito but also Peru and <b>Upper Peru</b>, which became Bolivia, and the union gave Bolívar the base for his wider plans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bolivia was created from <b>Upper Peru</b>, the territory of the Royal Audiencia of Charcas, whose silver mines at Potosí had made it one of the richest parts of the Spanish empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The silver mines of Potosí in <b>Upper Peru</b> (modern Bolivia), worked largely by indigenous labourers conscripted under the mita draft, and those of Zacatecas in Mexico, worked mainly by indigenous wage labour, made Spanish America the source of perhaps four-fifths of the world's silver between 1500 and 1800,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…former viceroy Santiago de Liniers was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and Montevideo swore to the Regency and the Viceroyalty of Peru annexed <b>Upper Peru</b>, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before any independence was declared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the wars of independence Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal turned Peru into the royalist base of South America, sending armies that crushed revolutionaries in <b>Upper Peru</b> and Quito and reconquered Chile after the Battle of Rancagua in 1814.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The text was fiercely pro-independence and anti-Spanish, and its outlook reached beyond Argentina to the struggles in Mexico, Central America, northern South America and <b>Charcas</b>, while its allegory cast not only Spanish absolutism but Spain itself as the enemy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/argentine-national-anthem">Argentine National Anthem</a></li></ul></section>
